## Boot

```sh
docker compose up --build
```

Open http://localhost:5173.
{% if locales | length > 1 %}
Choose English or Português (Brasil) in the header. Catalogs ship for every
selected locale.
{% elif locales[0] == 'en' %}
The landing is in English.
{% else %}
The landing is in Português (Brasil).
{% endif %}
The SPA proxies `/api`, `/auth`, and `/users` to FastAPI and keeps the browser
host, so local OAuth redirect URIs use that host. Postgres stores End-user
accounts. These containers are for local development. Stop with
`docker compose down`. Set `WEB_PORT` / `API_PORT` if the defaults are occupied.

Sign in with email and password from the header. The first registration works
with the Compose defaults: no OAuth app and no Redis. Access tokens are JWTs
that last one hour. Sign-out drops the token in the browser; the API cannot
revoke it, and a reload asks the End-user to sign in again. Password reset and
email verification are not mounted, because no mailer is configured.

Google and GitHub buttons appear only when both the client id and secret are
set (`GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID` / `GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET`, and the `GITHUB_`
pair). See `.env.example`. The browser returns to `/account` with the access
token in the URL fragment.

## Native development (equivalent)

Install Node 24, pnpm 12.9.1, uv, and Docker for Postgres.

```sh
pnpm install
uv sync
docker compose up -d db
pnpm nx run-many -t dev --parallel=2
```

Open http://localhost:5173. API docs: http://localhost:8000/docs.
The API reads `DATABASE_URL` and `SECRET` from the environment. When they are
unset it uses the same local Postgres URL and development secret as Compose.

```sh
pnpm nx run-many -t lint,typecheck,test,build
pnpm nx run api:lock
```

Python dependencies belong to uv; JS dependencies belong to pnpm. Commit the
`uv.lock` and `pnpm-lock.yaml` created at first install. Nx coordinates the
`api` and `web` projects. Email and password need no hosted credentials.
OAuth, payments, and CI are separate follow-ups. Replace `SECRET` before any
shared host. Copier records answers for inspection; automatic template updates
are not supported yet.
Landing copy and API error strings live in `locales/` as i18next JSON v4 catalogs.

## Production

Two origins: the Vite SPA on Cloudflare Pages, and FastAPI as a container on any
Docker host (Fly, Railway, a VPS, or equivalent). FastAPI does not serve the SPA
and does not run on Cloudflare Workers. Local Compose stays the development boot.

### API container

```sh
docker build -f Dockerfile.api -t {{ project_slug }}-api .
docker run --env-file .env -p 8000:8000 {{ project_slug }}-api
```

Put that process behind HTTPS. A Pages SPA can only call an `https://` API.
`Dockerfile.web` is the local Vite dev server; Pages hosts the static build.

### SPA on Cloudflare Pages

1. Deploy the API so `VITE_API_URL` is a live HTTPS origin.
2. Set `VITE_API_URL=https://<api-host>` in the environment that runs
   `pnpm nx run web:build`. Git integration: Pages **Settings → Environment
   variables** (production and preview separately). Direct Upload: set it in CI
   before `vite build`; dashboard vars do not rewrite an already-built bundle.
   Changing the API host requires a new build.
3. Git integration from this repo root: build command
   `pnpm install && pnpm nx run web:build`, output directory `apps/web/dist`,
   Node 24 (`NODE_VERSION=24`). The production branch updates
   `<project>.pages.dev` and custom domains; other branches get preview URLs.
4. Direct Upload: `pnpm nx run web:build` then
   `npx wrangler pages deploy apps/web/dist --project-name=<name>`.
5. Add every Pages hostname you attach (including `www` vs apex, and preview
   URLs that should call this API) to `CORS_ORIGINS` or `CORS_ORIGIN_REGEX`.
6. Leave out a top-level `404.html` so Pages serves `/` for unknown paths.
   Pages `_redirects` cannot proxy the API.

Use `apiUrl` in `apps/web/src/api.ts` for SPA API calls. With `VITE_API_URL`
unset, it returns same-origin `/api/...`, `/auth/...`, and `/users/...` URLs and
the Vite proxy forwards those requests. Send `Authorization: Bearer`; omit
`credentials: "include"` on `fetch`.

### Environment

Copy `.env.example`. `VITE_*` values are inlined into client JS at build time;
keep secrets on the API.

| Variable | Where | When |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `VITE_API_URL` | Pages / CI build | Pages deploy |
| `CORS_ORIGINS` | API runtime | Pages deploy (comma-separated origins, no paths) |
| `CORS_ORIGIN_REGEX` | API runtime | Optional preview hashes for this Pages project |
| `DATABASE_URL` | API runtime | API uses Postgres (Compose still uses `db`) |
| `SECRET` | API runtime | JWT signing; replace before any shared host |
| `SPA_ORIGIN` | API runtime | OAuth redirect back to the SPA |
| `REDIS_URL` | API runtime | Optional; omitted locally; JWT auth does not need it |
| `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY` | API runtime | When billing lands |
| `STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET` | API runtime | When billing lands |
| `GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID` | API runtime | Optional |
| `GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET` | API runtime | Optional |
| `GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID` | API runtime | Optional |
| `GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET` | API runtime | Optional |

`CORS_ORIGINS` is an explicit allowlist (`Authorization` and `Content-Type` are
already permitted). When billing lands, Stripe webhooks hit the API origin.
OAuth redirect URIs belong on the API origin (or the Vite proxy in local
Compose). Email/password works with the OAuth variables unset. Plans will live
in Postgres; Stripe keys stay in env.

## Agents

This tree ships `AGENTS.md` and `docs/agents/` for hosts that use Orca and the
Matt Pocock skills pack. Orca is optional: Compose boot and
`pnpm nx run-many -t lint,typecheck,test,build` do not invoke it.
